{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And said.</b> The Gaon [Saadia] says that the meaning of ויאמר is like וירצה [and He willed]. But if so, it should have been אור [\"light,\" without the imperative]. Rather, it is according to its plain meaning, as it is written, \"By the word of YHWH the heavens were made\" (Psalms 33:6), \"For He commanded and they were created\" (ibid. 148:5). The reason for the figure is about the act — that it was not with exertion — by way of a parable of a king and his attendants." "And this light was above the spirit."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "I read אור as Ibn Ezra's argument: if ויאמר meant 'He willed,' the verse should read simply אור rather than יהי אור. The parenthetical gloss is added only to clarify the grammatical point he is making."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:42:32.842166983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}